# Stacked pull requests

When splitting work into **dependent** PRs (a "stack"), use **GitHub native stacked pull requests** — not ad-hoc base-branch targeting, Graphite/spr/gt, or a single mega-PR.

## Workflow

Prefer the `gh stack` extension (`gh extension install github/gh-stack`; needs `gh` ≥ 2.90):

1. `gh stack init` — first layer off trunk (usually `main`)
2. Commit on the current layer; `gh stack add <branch>` (or `gh stack add -Am "..."`) for the next layer
3. `gh stack submit` — push and open/link PRs so each targets the layer below
4. `gh stack view` / `gh stack push` as needed; let GitHub rebase/retarget after merges

Keep each layer a focused, reviewable diff. Merge **bottom-up** (or merge a mid/top PR to land everything below it). Same-repo only; cross-fork stacks are unsupported.

## Do / don't

```text
# ✅ GOOD
gh stack init
# …commits on layer 1…
gh stack add feat/consumers-drawer
# …commits on layer 2…
gh stack submit

# ❌ BAD — manual "stack" without GitHub stacks
gh pr create --base feat/layer-1   # sibling branch, no stack link
# ❌ BAD — one huge PR, or Graphite/gt/spr when native stacks apply
```

PR title/body rules still apply per layer (`pr-conventional-commits`).
